cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "slotsync: slot and symbol timing recovery for OOK and PPM optical receivers"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-06
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Timing-error detectors for optical on-off-keyed and pulse-position-modulated
  receivers, with the chain from detector to loop design measured end to end:
  early-late, Gardner and Mueller-Mueller detectors as pure sample-level
  functions under one sign convention; exact S-curves computed by enumerating
  every data pattern within the pulse's finite support, so the detector gain K_d
  is the measured slope of a computed S-curve rather than a quoted figure; a
  second-order loop whose coefficients place the discrete poles exactly at the
  exponential image of the analogue prototype's, with the noise-bandwidth
  relation verified against quadrature; timing jitter variance by three
  independent routes - the classical white-noise closed form, the exact
  discrete-time Lyapunov solution, and the same loop driven by a measured
  detector-noise autocovariance - all compared against a closed-loop Monte Carlo
  with batch-means standard errors; a measured cycle-slip threshold alongside the
  classical Gaussian level-crossing estimate, which is reported to fail by six to
  sixteen orders of magnitude; and an M-ary PPM slot clock implemented as its own
  case, with a closed-form known answer and an explicit account of what does not
  carry over from binary symbol timing. Validation level 2, research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - timing-recovery
  - symbol-synchronization
  - slot-synchronization
  - gardner-detector
  - mueller-muller
  - early-late-gate
  - pulse-position-modulation
  - optical-communications
  - cycle-slip
  - phase-locked-loop
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Gardner
        given-names: "F. M."
    title: "A BPSK/QPSK Timing-Error Detector for Sampled Receivers"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Communications"
    volume: 34
    issue: 5
    start: 423
    end: 429
    year: 1986
    doi: "10.1109/TCOM.1986.1096561"
    notes: >-
      The source of the Gardner detector implemented in slotsync.detectors.
      Bibliographic details verified against the Crossref record for this DOI on
      2026-10-06. No page number, equation or numerical value from inside the
      paper is quoted anywhere in this repository.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Mueller
        given-names: "K. H."
      - family-names: Mueller
        given-names: "M."
    title: "Timing Recovery in Digital Synchronous Data Receivers"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Communications"
    volume: 24
    issue: 5
    start: 516
    end: 531
    year: 1976
    doi: "10.1109/TCOM.1976.1093326"
    notes: >-
      The source of the Mueller-Mueller detector. Bibliographic details verified
      against the Crossref record for this DOI on 2026-10-06. The arrangement in
      this repository carries the opposite sign, which the docstring states; the
      sign is a wiring convention.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Mengali
        given-names: "U."
      - family-names: "D'Andrea"
        given-names: "A. N."
    title: "Synchronization Techniques for Digital Receivers"
    publisher:
      name: "Springer"
    year: 1997
    isbn: "978-0-306-45725-8"
    notes: >-
      Named as the standard treatment of timing-error detectors and their
      S-curves. Bibliographic details verified against the publisher's catalogue
      record on 2026-10-06. Nothing in this repository quotes an equation from it.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Gardner
        given-names: "F. M."
    title: "Phaselock Techniques"
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2005
    isbn: "978-0-471-43063-6"
    notes: >-
      Named as the standard reference for loop noise and cycle slips in
      phase-locked loops. Bibliographic details verified against the publisher's
      catalogue record on 2026-10-06, whose chapter list includes "Effects of
      Additive Noise" and "Digital (Sampled) Phaselock Loops". No equation or page
      from it is used; the loop-noise bandwidth relation used here is verified
      numerically by validation/validate_loop_coefficients.py instead.
